=== step loading
title: Loading and errors
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
Opening a show takes a moment, and for that moment the show view shows the **previous** show, or nothing at all. And if the connection drops, nothing tells the person.

You solved both on the home page yesterday, in `loadShows`. The show view gets the same treatment, with its own elements: `showStatus` for the message and `showDetails` for the show itself.

```js
try {
  const data = await getWeather(city);
  renderWeather(data);
} catch (error) {
  weatherStatus.textContent = "Sorry, no weather right now.";
}
```

Scroll up to `loadShows` in your code if you need a reminder: the pattern is the same.
--- task
In `openShow`, after `window.scrollTo(0, 0);`:

1. Hide the details, `showDetails.hidden = true;`, and show `showStatus` with the text "Loading show…". Also remove its `error` class.
2. Put the `await` line and the three `render...` lines inside `try { }`. At the end of `try`, hide the status and show the details again.
3. In `catch (error) { }`, give `showStatus` a friendly error message and the class `error`.
--- hint
It's the same shape as `loadShows`: the message before `try`, the `await` in `try`, the error in `catch`.
--- hint
```js
async function openShow(id) {
  state.view = "show";
  render();
  window.scrollTo(0, 0);

  showDetails.hidden = true;
  showStatus.textContent = "Loading show…";
  showStatus.classList.remove("error");
  showStatus.hidden = false;

  try {
    const data = await getShowById(id);
    renderShow(data.show);
    renderCast(data.cast);
    renderSeasons(data.seasons);
    showStatus.hidden = true;
    showDetails.hidden = false;
  } catch (error) {
    showStatus.textContent = "Sorry, this show could not be loaded. Check your internet connection and try again.";
    showStatus.classList.add("error");
  }
}
```
--- solution-replace
